      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pelham takes its name from Thomas Pelham‑Holles, 1st Duke of Newcastle, a British nobleman who lived in the 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s southernmost point, marked by the Old Boundary Pine, once defined the northern edge of Massachusetts in the 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Founded in 1963, the Pelham Historical Society preserves artifacts and stories that trace the town’s early days and growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pelhamhistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pelham separated from Old Dunstable in 1741 after the Massachusetts‑New Hampshire border was finally settled.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Jeremy Hill, the town’s highest point, rises to 577 feet above sea level near Pelham’s western edge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Pelham is New Hampshire’s southernmost town and the easternmost in Hillsborough County, a unique geographic distinction.",
